00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:17.580
very beginning. Join me as we uncover the truth&nbsp;
behind the 2023 recession: aka the great reset.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.960
RECESSION DEBUNKED:
First of all, we are not in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.840
recession. Economically speaking, a recession&nbsp;
is defined as 2 quarters of GDP decline. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.180
is the graph of America’s GDP over the past 5&nbsp;
years. Clearly, there has been no such declines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:42.960
since the beginning of the pandemic. But, wait a&nbsp;
minute, weren’t we in a mild recession in 2022. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.640
swear there were articles claiming that we were&nbsp;
officially in a recession. Well, there actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.260
wasn’t any articles that were claiming that. If&nbsp;
we set our search time frame to the second half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.000
of last year and look up “US enters a recession”,&nbsp;
we’ll see that every single reputable news outlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.460
framed the the topic as a question as opposed to&nbsp;
a statement of fact. The ones that were claiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:10.020
it as fact were actually just YouTubers. Now,&nbsp;
there was some truth to what they were saying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:16.500
What these guys were looking at was real GDP or&nbsp;
inflation adjusted GDP where there was indeed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:24.300
substantial decline from $20 trillion to $19.9&nbsp;
trillion or a whopping 0.5%. As you can see, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:30.180
decline was virtually nothing. For perspective,&nbsp;
the covid recession was nearly 10% but that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:35.160
just the most blatant issue with calling this a&nbsp;
recession. For starters, even by the traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:41.760
definition, a recession refers to nominal GDP&nbsp;
decline not inflation adjusted GDP decline. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.740
is why the 2020 recession is highlighted with&nbsp;
a gray bar while the supposed 2022 recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:53.760
is not. But more importantly, this rule is just a&nbsp;
general idea. If there’s two consecutive quarters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:04:00.780
of GDP decline, then hey, maybe the economy is not&nbsp;
doing so great. But, the reality is that there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:06.780
so many more factors that are needed to gauge&nbsp;
real economic health than just GDP. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.940
you may also wanna consider the unemployment&nbsp;
rate, non farm payroll, industrial production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:17.280
and retail sales just to name a few. Here’s&nbsp;
a graph of unemployment for example. Nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:24.420
at all time lows. So, objectively speaking, we&nbsp;
were never in a recession in 2022 but what about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:30.900
looking forward? What about the 2023 and 2024&nbsp;
recession and the great reset? Well, everyday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.940
that’s looking less and less likely as well. For&nbsp;
a recession to happen, we would first need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.280
build up some negative momentum. You know, things&nbsp;
like increasing unemployment, more layoffs, lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.880
salaries, and so on. But not only is this not&nbsp;
happening, but the exact opposite is happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.880
We’re gaining positive momentum. Layoffs have&nbsp;
substantially slowed down with the peak being back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:00.240
in January. In fact, the layoffs this June were&nbsp;
actually lower than layoffs last June. Payroll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.940
growth has also gained some positive momentum with&nbsp;
April and May being positive months. Also, as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.860
touched on at the beginning, inflation has cooled&nbsp;
off quite well and blue chip stocks are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:16.560
better than anyone expected. With that being said,&nbsp;
the general fear was simply a case of the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.920
public eating up media and YouTube fear mongering&nbsp;
but what about big tech stocks. Initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.500
these got destroyed due to big money selling off.&nbsp;
These guys clearly have more insight than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.560
general public right. Why were they so scared of a&nbsp;
potential recession and especially interest rates?

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.980
INTEREST RATE HIKES DEBUNKED:
By far the number one thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:46.740
was referenced as the big tech company killer was&nbsp;
rising interest rates. Basically, the entirety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:52.200
of big tech happened within the past 15 years&nbsp;
during which interest rates were literally 0. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.760
there was a lot of anxiety regarding how high&nbsp;
interest rates would affect these massive growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:02.400
companies. But there’s just one fatal flaw with&nbsp;
this concern. Most of the biggest tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.680
in the world are no longer growth companies to&nbsp;
begin with. Yes, they still put up insane growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.740
numbers year after year but that’s not at the cost&nbsp;
of profit. Traditional growth companies sacrifice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:19.860
profitability to achieve growth. Likely the best&nbsp;
examples are Uber and all the food delivery apps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:26.220
But, this is not the case with Google or Apple&nbsp;
or Facebook or Microsoft. I’m sure you already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:31.680
know how profitable these companies are but lets&nbsp;
go through their annual net profit for fun. Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:40.920
$112 billion. Microsoft, $85 billion. And Google,&nbsp;
$71 billion. Do you really think these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:48.180
are suddenly at risk due to interest rates rising&nbsp;
from 2% to 5%? Of course not and this has become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.160
abundantly clear within the past 12 months.&nbsp;
Take Apple for example. They have a total of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.920
$100 billion in debt. For one, most of this debt&nbsp;
was locked in year ago, so it’s not like Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:04.140
is having to pay a whole lot more all of a sudden.&nbsp;
But even if we assume that all of Apple’s debt was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:09.540
affected by the interest rate hikes, it’s not&nbsp;
that big of a deal. Even if Apple’s rates got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:17.160
hiked from 2% up to 7%, we’re only talking about&nbsp;
an extra $5 billion. It’s crazy to refer to that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:24.240
as only but at Apple’s scale, it truly is just&nbsp;
only. With $112 billion worth of annual profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:30.720
an extra $5 billion would only reduce their&nbsp;
profitability by 4.5% and that’s if the interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.280
hit their bottom line directly. You can bet that&nbsp;
they’re gonna write the interest off on taxes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:41.520
so the real cost to profitability would be like&nbsp;
3%. In other words, most of the big tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.860
are completely unaffected by the rate hikes.&nbsp;
Now, if we’re talking about SPACs and hype plays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.700
that’s a wholenother story, but to be&nbsp;
honest, those deserved to get wrecked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.540
Companies with strong fundamentals really had no&nbsp;
issues but that’s only half the puzzle. The other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.920
argument was that investors would no longer be&nbsp;
interested in these companies. When you can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.680
5% guaranteed by lending to the government or&nbsp;
7% by lending to big corporations with bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.560
why in the world would you invest in stocks?&nbsp;
Well, this does have some truth to it. If you’re a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.720
conservative investor, the answer is no reason at&nbsp;
all. Statistically speaking, the average investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:24.540
has no chance of beating average market returns of&nbsp;
7 to 8%. So, if you can get nearly the same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:30.420
with bonds, why invest in something that’s far&nbsp;
riskier? Well, the answer is that that’s literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:36.960
the job of most of big money. While they suck at&nbsp;
beating the market, their job is to indeed try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.240
beat the market. The best way to do that over the&nbsp;
past 10 years was to invest in big tech. Will this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:48.360
work again? Well, no one knows especially with&nbsp;
AI making big leaps. But, one things for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:54.660
a lot of big money is rotating back into tech&nbsp;
despite all the fear. But if there’s no recession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:00.000
and big tech companies are doing just fine,&nbsp;
why in the world did we see so many layoffs?

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.480
LAYOFFS DEBUNKED:&nbsp;

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:08.940
Naturally, everyone associates layoffs with&nbsp;
recessions. So, when even the biggest names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.860
started laying off, everyone assumed that we were&nbsp;
surely in a recession or that we were heading into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:20.520
one shortly but that’s not actually the case. You&nbsp;
see, much of the layoffs weren’t caused by some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.640
sort of macro economic trend but rather micro&nbsp;
economic trends within tech. By far the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.500
factor throwing things off balance over the past&nbsp;
few years was Covid. When Covid showed up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.180
everyone hunkered down, they naturally flocked to&nbsp;
things that they could do at home. This meant more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.520
time spent on Netflix, social media, smartphones,&nbsp;
computers, and the internet in general. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.740
the demand for these products skyrocketed. In&nbsp;
fact, many tech companies had to scale their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:51.840
operations specifically to keep up with this&nbsp;
demand. The problem was that many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.640
companies assumed that this was the new normal&nbsp;
and that demand would continue to growing at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:03.120
rate. As such, they hired a crap ton of employees.&nbsp;
Google’s employee count exploded from 118,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:10.380
to 190,000. Facebook’s employee count exploded&nbsp;
from 45,000 to 86,000. And Amazon’s employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:17.220
count exploded from 800,000 to 1.6 million. As&nbsp;
you might guess, these guys were in for quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:22.560
a rude awakening when people started spending&nbsp;
money elsewhere. On eating out, vacations, music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:27.780
festivals, physical stores, transportation, and&nbsp;
so on. People never stopped spending money, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:33.540
why our GDP has continued to grow. But people did&nbsp;
pull back on their tech intake whether that be in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.940
terms of time or money. This was especially true&nbsp;
for pandemic specific tech companies like Zoom who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:44.220
have simply gotten obliterated. At the same time&nbsp;
that growth rates were slowing down, productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:49.860
numbers were also slowing down. You see, all of&nbsp;
this crazy hiring naturally brought in a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:54.780
sub par people into these companies. Existing&nbsp;
employees also become a lot less productive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:01.680
because they had 10 job offers waiting outside, so&nbsp;
they were by no means scared of getting fired. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.440
also had all these movements like quiet quitting.&nbsp;
Basically, all of this simply meant that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.020
companies were dealing with more rest and vesters&nbsp;
than ever. For obvious reasons, this put companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:18.960
at quite a big hiring disadvantage. Not only were&nbsp;
they constantly having to offer larger and larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.360
compensation packages but the employees that&nbsp;
they were hiring were less and less effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:29.880
It was a lose lose scenario for these companies.&nbsp;
Fortunately for them, layoffs gave them the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:34.920
perfect way to work their way out of this vicious&nbsp;
cycle. So, all these layoffs that we read about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:40.380
on the news aren’t really indicative of the macro&nbsp;
economic climate. It’s really just indicative of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.960
the tech world which did indeed have a correction&nbsp;
coming off the pandemic high. Zooming out though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:51.360
we’re not in a recession but a recession&nbsp;
is inevitable. With that being said though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:57.660
no one knows when that’s gonna be. When everyone&nbsp;
thinks that it’s gonna be in 6 or 12 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:04.080
that’s how you know that it’s definitely not&nbsp;
going to be in 6 or 12 months. You can’t expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:09.900
a recession. If you expect a recession, you’ll do&nbsp;
things to prepare for the downturn which will in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:15.060
turn prevent the recession from even happening.&nbsp;
Recessions happen when the general public is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:21.840
overconfident in the market which is definitely&nbsp;
not the case right now. So, the best option is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:26.880
just forget trying to predict the next recession&nbsp;
and stick to a strong investment plan that works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:33.420
over the long term whether that be buying the S&amp;P&nbsp;
500 or investing in bonds but that’s just what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:38.700
think. Someone that’s actually facing a recession&nbsp;
is TikTokers. Check out this video to learn more.

